Here's a complete example of an MCP server with tools wrapping a REST API:

---

## Tool Class (`ApiTools.cs`)

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class ApiTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<ApiTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Fetch a user by their ID from the REST API.")]
    public async Task<string> GetUser(
        [Description("The unique user ID")] int userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching user {UserId}", userId);
        var response = await httpClient.GetAsync($"/users/{userId}", cancellationToken);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Search users by name. Returns matching user records.")]
    public async Task<string> SearchUsers(
        [Description("Name or partial name to search for")] string query,
        [Description("Max results to return (1-50)")] int limit = 10,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Searching users: {Query}", query);
        var response = await httpClient.GetAsync(
            $"/users?name={Uri.EscapeDataString(query)}&limit={limit}", cancellationToken);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }
}
```

---

## `Program.cs` (stdio transport)

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: Log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC transport
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

// Register HttpClient with a base address for your API
builder.Services.AddHttpClient<ApiTools>(client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.example.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

The SDK integrates with `Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection` seamlessly:

| Pattern | How it works |
|---------|--------------|
| **Constructor injection** | Non-static `[McpServerToolType]` classes are resolved from DI. Use primary constructors to receive `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`, etc. |
| **Method parameter injection** | Any parameter whose type is registered in DI (and isn't a tool argument) is injected automatically and **hidden from the tool schema**. |
| **Auto-hidden types** | `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<T>`, and all DI services never appear in the JSON schema the LLM sees. |

The LLM only sees parameters decorated with `[Description]` — everything else is infrastructure injected by the framework.

**Registration flow:**
1. `builder.Services.AddHttpClient<ApiTools>(...)` → registers typed `HttpClient`
2. `.WithToolsFromAssembly()` → discovers `[McpServerToolType]` classes and registers them
3. At invocation time, the SDK resolves the class from DI, injecting constructor dependencies

This means your tools stay focused on business logic while the framework handles wiring.
